*** bmc2_tree -- calculating distances between gene and species trees

Examples of usage [1]:

$ bmc2_tree gene.tre species.tre
$ bmc2_tree species.tre species.tre
$ bmc2_tree gene.tre gene.tre 
(but "bmc2_tree species.tre gene.tre" is not allowed for these files")

_______________

This example contains two tree files, one representing species and the other a gene family. Each file contains 50 trees,
and the program bmc_tree will compare all possible 2500 pairs, giving us several reconciliation measures as well some
other distance measures in the file "pairwise.txt". The screen output is somewhat obscure -- it gives the distances
divided by categories -- and can be safely ignored.

The difference between the gene tree and the species tree are two:
1) the species names must be contained within the gene names, where they can be the same; not all species must be
represented in the gene family (e.g. if we have loss).
2) all gene tree leaves must have a correspondence with some species. IOW if the species are 

The mapping is done by matching exactly each species name against each gene name, where we start with the longer species
names (to avoid for instance mapping the species ECO to a gene ECOLI despite there is a species named ECOL). The program
complains if at the end some gene was not mapped to any species.

Notes:

[1] There are other usages of the program, that are not so interesting:
  a) if there is only a single file, the program will simulate trees on same leafset and at the end compare the
frequency of simulated trees with those at the file. Therefore the file should have e.g. uniformly sampled trees from a
leafset or *all* possible trees for that leafset. And then we can check if our tree randomization functions (like SPR
swap, or star-refinement random sampling) are adequate for the MCMC, or if some trees/shapes are favored, etc. 
  b) if besides the gene and species tree we also give the program a number, it will use it as the number of samples on
a Monte Carlo integration of (an old version of) the normalization constant for the reconciliation probability function. 
In this case it assumes that the gene tree has all possible trees s.t. it can calculate the "true" normalization constants.  

[2] This output helped me in an analysis where I wanted to see how the reconciliations were related (e.g. losses are
certainly not independent from duplications), and thus tried to describe the "histogram" of triplets 
(dups, losses, deepcoals)



